<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Almost everything with a deadline arrives by email, and then drowns.
    The inspection date, the renewal notice, the letter that needed a reply
    within thirty days — all technically in your inbox, alongside four
    thousand things that aren't.
  </p>
  <p>
    Connecting a mailbox lets the app read your mail down onto your own
    disk, so it can notice the things that matter and you can ask questions
    of years of correspondence.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What it's for</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Asking your own mail things") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        "When did the landlord say the inspection was?" — answered from your
        actual correspondence, with the message it came from, rather than
        from your memory of it.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "One calm summary a day") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        What actually mattered across every mailbox you've connected. A day
        with nothing important in it sends nothing, which is the part most
        digests get wrong.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The genuinely urgent thing") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A cancelled flight, a moved court date. Rare by design — an alert
        that fires often is an alert nobody reads.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The reply that never came") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Something you sent has gone a week without an answer, and you'd
        forgotten. A nudge, and a follow-up ready to send.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">It talks to your provider, and nobody else</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The app connects directly to your mail host — anything speaking the
    standard protocol, which is effectively all of them. Your mail moves
    from your provider to your machine. There is no service in the middle,
    ours or anyone's, and the model reading it is the one running in your
    house.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    You can pause the whole thing, delete what's been copied down, and
    disconnect an account — and doing so removes the local copy rather than
    hiding it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Mail is hostile input, and is treated as such</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Anyone can send you an email, which means anyone can put text in front
    of your assistant. That's an unusual risk and it's handled deliberately:
    message content is treated as something to read about, never as
    instructions to follow. An email telling the app to do something is an
    email that said a strange thing, not a command.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">It teaches quickly</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Tell it once that this sender never matters, and it stops raising them.
    The rules you've taught are visible and editable, so triage becomes
    something you shaped rather than something happening to you.
  </p>
</section>
